    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d689">

                <pb facs="0663" n="659" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg659"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/514: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-17T18:00:00+09:00">February 17, 1938—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 17—8:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>112. Department’s 59, of February 15, 6 p.m., Chinese customs.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The considerations outlined by the Department were imparted to the British
                        Ambassador today.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Craigie was informed recently by the Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs
                        that revised instructions have been sent to Okamoto<anchor n="65"
                            xml:id="d689-f1"/> which Horinouchi believes will be more acceptable to
                        foreign interests. These instructions envisage <hi rendition="#italic">inter
                            alia</hi> the deposit in the Yokohama Specie Bank only of the customs
                        revenues taken in districts now under Japanese occupation, all other customs
                        revenues to be deposited in the Hong Kong [and] Shanghai Bank. If
                            Lawford<anchor n="66" xml:id="d689-f2"/> declines to accept the new
                        proposal, Okamoto is, according to Craigie, directed to discuss the matter
                        with the foreign representatives in Shanghai. Horinouchi implied that these
                        instructions might be even further amended if they fail to meet the views of
                        the foreign interests.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Our <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> was presented to the Foreign
                        Office this afternoon.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d689-f3"/></item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai for Hankow and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
